{%- comment -%}
  AJ Tee of the Week — one graphic tee, slabbed like a graded collector card.

  A CARD RATHER THAN A DEAL, and the discount arithmetic is the reason. Measured
  on 4 August against the live catalogue: 145 graphic tees, median price $46.00
  against a $27.60 cost, so a 40% margin. Hold a 20% margin floor and a 15% cut
  leaves 93 of the 145 eligible — nearly two years of weekly picks. A 25% cut
  leaves 18, which is four months and then repeats.

  So 15% is the only sustainable number, and 15% is the problem: the Bargain Bin
  two sections up is a permanent 25%, and a shallower discount presented AS a
  discount reads as the worse offer sitting above the better one. A slabbed card
  sidesteps the comparison entirely — it sells scarcity and curation, which is
  what a weekly pick actually is, and lets the 15% arrive as a corner sticker
  rather than the headline. Nothing here claims to beat the Bin.

  THE SLAB, NOT JUST A CARD, and that came out of the graded-card mockup: a
  cream label band carrying the set name and a serial, then the card itself,
  the whole assembly inside a rigid clear case. A bare card is a bordered
  rectangle. A card somebody sealed in plastic is a thing that was judged worth
  protecting, which is the entire argument this section makes. It costs three
  wrapper elements and no image assets.

  NO GRADE NUMBER, and that omission is deliberate rather than an oversight.
  The obvious move is to put a big "9.5" where a graded card carries its grade,
  and it would look terrific — but a grade is a claim about condition, and a
  made-up one on a live storefront is a small lie printed in the largest type on
  the page. The slot holds the week number instead, which is true, sits in the
  same place, and reads the same at a glance.

  THE PERCENTAGE IS COMPUTED, NEVER TYPED. The sticker renders only when the
  variant genuinely carries a compare-at above its price, and prints whatever
  the real gap is. If nobody discounts the product this week the card still
  works — it is a pick, not a sale — and no number appears.

  PICKED BY AN AGENT, ORDERED BY HAND. The product picker wins if it is set;
  otherwise the first product of the tee collection is used, which is what the
  Tee of the Week agent maintains. Clearing the picker hands control back to the
  queue without touching this file.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings

  # The picker wins, so a manual override never needs a deploy or an agent pause.
  #
  # A HANDLE, NOT A COLLECTION PICKER, and the reason cost a deploy. A
  # `collection` setting makes Shopify resolve the handle to a real collection
  # when it VALIDATES the template — and this collection is created by the app,
  # so on a fresh install it does not exist yet. The whole of index.json was
  # rejected server-side and the section silently never appeared. Every other
  # app-maintained collection in this theme is referenced by handle for exactly
  # this reason; see `collection_handle` in aj-bargain-bin.
  #
  # Resolve the handle FIRST. Liquid does not allow a filter inside a bracket
  # lookup, so `collections[s.tee_collection | default: 'x']` silently yields
  # nil — the same trap aj-drawer-picks documents.
  assign tee = s.tee_product
  if tee == blank
    assign tee_handle = s.tee_collection | default: 'tee-of-the-week'
    assign tee_collection = collections[tee_handle]
    if tee_collection != blank
      assign tee = tee_collection.products.first
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if tee != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    EVERY FIGURE ON THE CARD IS DERIVED IN THE SNIPPET NOW — the week number off
    the calendar, the size range off the size option, the serial off the SKU. It
    was computed here while the card was inline; leaving a second copy behind
    would have been the exact drift this extraction was meant to prevent.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    class="aj-teew color-{{ s.color_scheme }}"
    style="padding-block-start: {{ s.padding-block-start }}px; padding-block-end: {{ s.padding-block-end }}px;"
    {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    <div class="aj-teew__inner page-width">
      {%- comment -%}
        The slab leads on desktop and the copy sits beside it, because the slab
        IS the argument — a paragraph explaining that a t-shirt is collectible
        persuades nobody, and a sealed numbered card does it without a sentence.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% render 'aj-tee-slab',
        tee: tee,
        series: s.card_series,
        subset: s.card_subset,
        window_style: s.window_style,
        art: s.tee_image,
        series_start: s.series_start
      %}

      {%- comment -%}
        The copy and the archive link share a column wrapper so the grid row is
        sized by the CARD rather than by the text. Spanning the card across two
        rows instead — the previous attempt — handed the row height to the copy,
        and the card then hung out of the band by half its own height.

        On a phone this wrapper becomes `display: contents`, so the copy and the
        link drop into the parent flex as separate items and can be ordered
        around the card independently. One element, two behaviours, one link.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="aj-teew__side">
      <div class="aj-teew__copy">
        {%- comment -%}
          SHIPS FREE, WHEN IT DOES — AND UP HERE, NOT BESIDE THE BUY BUTTON.

          It sat between the body copy and "Yoink it" until 11 Aug 2026, where
          it failed twice over. The pill is #ffe000 and the button is #ffde00,
          so two near-identical yellows stacked and read as one shape — the mark
          looked like a tab welded to the button rather than a fact about the
          offer. And it repeated the exact mistake this file already fixed for
          the archive link, whose own comment records that anything sitting next
          to "Yoink it" reads as a second, competing call to action.

          `aj-ships-free.css` says the mark belongs WITH THE PRICE, because free
          shipping is arithmetic rather than a hook. On this section the price is
          inside the rendered card image, so there is no number here to sit
          beside — which is how it ended up orphaned against the CTA. Pairing it
          with the kicker makes it a statement about the week's offer, which is
          the nearest honest reading available in this column.

          THE EXACT TEST THE OTHER SECTIONS USE, and it must stay exact: a real
          `draftOrderCalculate` quote of zero, never the vendor's general shape.
          A supplier who ships most things free can still charge on one item.

          `| plus: 0` because `ship.cost` is a `number_decimal` and arrives as a
          STRING — "0.0" == 0 is false in Liquid, which is why the deal section's
          first attempt rendered nothing on a product that did ship free.

          The blank check stays on the METAFIELD rather than the coerced number:
          `blank | plus: 0` is 0, so testing the coerced value would announce
          free shipping on every tee nobody has measured.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign tee_ships_free = false -%}
        {%- assign tee_ship_mf = tee.metafields.ship.cost -%}
        {%- if tee_ship_mf != blank -%}
          {%- assign tee_ship = tee_ship_mf.value | plus: 0 -%}
          {%- if tee_ship == 0 -%}
            {%- assign tee_ships_free = true -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The row renders when EITHER part is present, so a shop that clears the
          kicker still gets the shipping mark, and a tee that charges shipping
          still gets the kicker. Wrapping only when both exist would drop one of
          them silently.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if s.kicker != blank or tee_ships_free -%}
          <div class="aj-teew__eyebrow">
            {%- if s.kicker != blank -%}
              <p class="aj-teew__kicker">{{ s.kicker | escape }}</p>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- if tee_ships_free -%}
              <p class="aj-card-free aj-teew__ship">Ships free</p>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if s.heading != blank -%}
          <h2 class="aj-teew__heading">{{ s.heading | escape }}</h2>
          {%- comment -%}
            The same four colours in the same order as the card's own band. Two
            halves of a section sitting on a flat ground read as two things; one
            repeated element makes them one.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="aj-teew__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if s.body != blank -%}
          <p class="aj-teew__body">{{ s.body | escape }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          A sold-out pick is a real possibility on a section that holds one
          product for seven days, and a live buy button on a dead product is
          worse than an honest label.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          TWO ACTIONS, ONE OF THEM ALWAYS AVAILABLE. Buying is the primary and
          it disappears when the tee sells out — but "see the rest of the set"
          is the more interesting click for anyone who came for the collecting
          rather than the shirt, and it is the ONLY thing left to offer when
          the week's card is gone. So the archive link sits outside the
          availability branch and renders either way.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The shipping mark used to render here. It now sits with the kicker at
          the top of this column — see the note there for why.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="aj-teew__actions">
          {%- if tee.available -%}
            <a class="aj-teew__cta" href="{{ tee.url }}">
              {{- s.cta_label | escape -}}
              <span aria-hidden="true">&nbsp;&rarr;</span>
            </a>
          {%- else -%}
            <p class="aj-teew__gone">{{ s.sold_out_label | escape }}</p>
          {%- endif -%}

        </div>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        A SIBLING OF THE CARD, NOT A NEIGHBOUR OF THE BUY BUTTON. Sat next to
        "Yoink it" on a phone it read as a second, competing call to action —
        two things to press, one of which does not buy anything. Out here it can
        be placed under the copy on desktop and below the card on a phone, from
        one element and one link.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if s.archive_label != blank and s.archive_link != blank -%}
        <a class="aj-teew__cta2" href="{{ s.archive_link }}">
          {{- s.archive_label | escape -}}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The cursor tracking that used to sit here is now `assets/aj-tee-slab.js`,
    loaded by the snippet. Same behaviour, and still progressive enhancement:
    everything above works without it, since the slab lifts, straightens and
    catches the light on hover in pure CSS.
  {%- endcomment -%}
{%- endif -%}

{{ 'aj-ships-free.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{% stylesheet %}
  /*
    The band is purple and the pill is drawn for a light ground, so it gets its
    own pairing here rather than inheriting one that would put cyan on purple.
    Yellow carrying ink is the arrangement aj-brand.css permits, and it matches
    the price stickers on the merch cards.
  */
  /*
    The eyebrow row: kicker and shipping mark on one line, wrapping to two on a
    narrow column rather than squeezing. The 0.5rem the kicker used to carry
    moves here, so the gap under the row is unchanged whether the mark renders
    or not — a section that shifted vertically depending on one product's
    shipping cost would read as a layout bug.
  */
  .aj-teew__eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .aj-teew__eyebrow .aj-teew__kicker {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .aj-teew__ship {
    margin: 0;
    --aj-ships-free-bg: #ffe000;
    --aj-ships-free-ink: #282828;
  }

  /*
    A PURPLE BAND, and the choice was made by what the homepage had already
    spent. `aj-how` runs cyan and `aj-story` runs yellow, so a third section in
    either turns the page into stripes of the same two colours. Purple is the
    remaining brand colour with enough contrast to hold white type, and it is
    the one that reads as a display case rather than a banner, which is the
    whole idea here. A cream slab on purple is also the highest-contrast
    pairing available — the card has to be the first thing seen.
  */
  /*
    A THIN BAND THE CARD BREAKS OUT OF. The slab overhangs the purple top and
    bottom, which is the one thing here that cannot be done by any stock
    section — and it is why `overflow` is visible rather than hidden. Hidden
    would clip precisely the overhang that makes the effect.

    `position: relative` and a z-index are load-bearing, not tidiness. Sections
    paint in source order, so without them the NEXT section down (Shop by
    price) would paint its white background over the bottom of the card while
    the top half hung correctly over the Bargain Bin — an asymmetry that reads
    as a rendering bug rather than a design.

    Cyan hairlines top and bottom. Cyan is legal here as a rule but never as
    type on this ground — see the contrast note in aj-how.
  */
  .aj-teew {
    --aj-teew-cream: #f2ead9;
    --aj-teew-cream-2: #e4d8c0;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    background: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    color: #ffffff;
    overflow: visible;
    border-block: 3px solid var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8);

    /*
      CLEARANCE FOR THE OVERHANG. The card hangs out of the band by design, so
      the band needs room around it or the card lands ON the neighbouring
      section — it was overlapping the Bargain Bin's own button. This is margin
      rather than padding on purpose: padding would grow the purple back, which
      is the exact opposite of the thin strip we wanted. The margin puts empty
      white either side for the card to hang into.
    */
    margin-block: 5rem;
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .aj-teew {
      /* Only the bottom overhangs on a phone, so only the bottom needs the room. */
      margin-block: 2rem 3.5rem;
    }
  }

  .aj-teew__inner {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
    justify-items: center;
    max-width: 64rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew__inner {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: center;
      /* Column gap only. The row gap here would push the archive link away from
         the button it belongs under, since the two are now in adjacent rows. */
      column-gap: 3.5rem;
      row-gap: 0;
      justify-items: stretch;
    }
  }

  /* ---- where the slab SITS -----------------------------------------------

     The slab itself now lives in `assets/aj-tee-slab.css`, because the tee's
     product page renders the same card and snippets cannot carry a
     stylesheet tag. What stays here is the part that is about this band:
     the breakout margins, and the `.aj-teew__side` / `.aj-teew__cta2` rules that
     happen to share these media queries.

     THE STAGE RULES BELOW ARE SCOPED `.aj-teew .aj-teew__stage`, and the extra
     class is the whole point rather than decoration. The snippet emits its own
     <link> where it renders, i.e. in the body, which lands AFTER this section's
     compiled stylesheet — so on equal specificity the asset would win. Both
     files set `max-width` on the stage (22rem there, 19rem here on a phone), and
     with source order deciding, the phone card would have quietly grown by 3rem.
     One extra class settles it by specificity instead, in the direction we want:
     this file owns where the slab sits, the asset owns what it is.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /*
    THE BREAKOUT, and it is negative margin rather than a transform on purpose.
    A translate would move the card without shortening the row, so the band
    would stay tall and the card would simply sit high inside it. Negative block
    margins shrink the row the card occupies, so the BAND gets thinner and the
    card keeps its size — which is the actual request.

    The overhang is the difference between this and the section's own padding.
    At 24px padding and -4.5rem here, the card clears the purple by about 3rem
    at each edge. Raising the padding sliders in the editor eats into that
    before it eats into anything else, which is the right order.

    DESKTOP ONLY. On a phone the copy stacks above the slab, and pulling the
    slab up by 4.5rem would drive it into the button rather than out of the
    band. The phone gets a smaller, bottom-only breakout below.
  */
  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew .aj-teew__stage {
      margin-block: -3.25rem;
    }

    .aj-teew__side {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }

    .aj-teew__cta2 {
      margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
    }
  }


  /* ---- the copy column ---------------------------------------------------- */

  .aj-teew__copy {
    text-align: center;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew__copy {
      text-align: start;
    }
  }

  .aj-teew__kicker {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
  }

  .aj-teew__rule {
    display: block;
    width: 8rem;
    height: 5px;
    margin: 0 auto 1rem;
    border-radius: 2px;
    /*
      INK WHERE THE CARD USES PURPLE. The card's band sits on cream, where all
      four brand colours read. This one sits on the purple ground, and rendered
      with the card's exact palette the third segment simply vanished — a
      four-colour bar with a hole in it. Ink is the substitution that keeps the
      rhythm without inventing a fifth brand colour.
    */
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      var(--aj-brand-cyan, #5cd8f8) 0 25%,
      var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000) 25% 50%,
      var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828) 50% 75%,
      var(--aj-brand-teal, #30aac6) 75% 100%
    );
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew__rule {
      margin-inline: 0;
    }
  }

  .aj-teew__heading {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-family: "Flashy", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6.5vw, 3.25rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .aj-teew__body {
    margin: 0 auto 1.4rem;
    max-width: 36ch;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.86);
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew__body {
      margin-inline: 0;
    }
  }

  .aj-teew__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.75rem 1rem;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .aj-teew__actions {
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }
  }

  .aj-teew__cta {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.85rem 1.6rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: translate 0.15s ease, box-shadow 0.15s ease;
  }

  .aj-teew__cta:hover {
    translate: 0 -2px;
    box-shadow: 0 10px 20px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.28);
  }

  /*
    A LINK, NOT A SECOND BUTTON. Two filled buttons side by side make a reader
    choose before they know what either does, and this one is the lesser of the
    two on a section whose job is to sell the tee in front of them. Underlined
    rather than outlined so it reads as somewhere to go rather than something
    to press.
  */
  .aj-teew__cta2 {
    display: inline-block;
    justify-self: center;
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #ffffff;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .aj-teew__cta2:hover {
    color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
  }

  .aj-teew__gone {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Ohpixel", var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.66);
  }


  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .aj-teew__inner {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 1.6rem;
    }

    /*
      The copy leads on a phone. Stacked in source order the slab would come
      first and push the heading below the fold, so the reader meets an
      unlabelled object. On desktop both are visible at once and the slab leads.
    */
    .aj-teew__copy {
      order: -1;
    }

    /*
      A BOTTOM-ONLY BREAKOUT ON A PHONE. The copy stacks above the card here, so
      pulling the top out would push the slab into the button rather than
      through the purple. The bottom edge still gets crossed, which is enough to
      read as deliberate.
    */
    .aj-teew .aj-teew__stage {
      max-width: 19rem;
      margin-block-end: -2.5rem;
    }

    /*
      The wrapper stops existing as a box, so its two children become flex items
      of the section itself and can be ordered around the card.
    */
    .aj-teew__side {
      display: contents;
    }

    /*
      LAST, BELOW THE CARD. The order it sits in is the order of intent: read
      the pitch, look at the tee, buy it — and only then, if none of that landed,
      go and browse the rest of the set.
    */
    .aj-teew__cta2 {
      order: 1;
      margin-block-start: 3.25rem;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "AJ Tee of the Week",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "One graphic tee a week, presented as a graded collector card. The week number increments from the series start date on its own — nothing to bump."
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "This week's tee" },
    { "type": "product", "id": "tee_product", "label": "Tee" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "tee_collection",
      "label": "Fallback collection handle",
      "info": "Used only when no tee is picked above — takes the first product. Must match the collection the app maintains.",
      "default": "tee-of-the-week"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "tee_image",
      "label": "Cut-out image",
      "info": "Optional. A background-free PNG of the tee, square, centred, with a little padding. Overrides the product's own image on this card only."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "window_style",
      "label": "Card window",
      "info": "Photo fills the frame — right for tees shot on concrete, which is most of them. Cut-out floats a background-free PNG on a halftone field. White suits stock shot on white.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "photo", "label": "Photo (fills the frame)" },
        { "value": "cutout", "label": "Cut-out on halftone" },
        { "value": "white", "label": "Plain white" }
      ],
      "default": "photo"
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Copy" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "kicker", "label": "Kicker", "default": "One a week" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Tee of the Week" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "body",
      "label": "Text",
      "default": "One graphic tee, pulled from the pile and sealed up for seven days. Then it goes back and a new one takes its place."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_label", "label": "Button", "default": "Yoink it" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "archive_label",
      "label": "Archive link text",
      "info": "Shows whether or not this week's tee is in stock — when it sells out this is the only thing left to click.",
      "default": "See the whole set"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "archive_link",
      "label": "Archive link",
      "info": "A plain path, not a picker — the agent creates the archive collection, so a picker would refuse to resolve it before the first rotation. Blank hides the link, which is what page.tee-of-the-week does since that page already shows the set.",
      "default": "/pages/tee-of-the-week"
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "sold_out_label", "label": "Sold-out text", "default": "Gone — back next week with a new one" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Card" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "card_series", "label": "Label set name", "default": "The Yoink" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "card_subset", "label": "Label subset", "default": "Tee of the Week" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "series_start",
      "label": "Series start date",
      "info": "YYYY-MM-DD. Week 1 begins here; the number climbs by one every seven days.",
      "default": "2026-08-04"
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Appearance" },
    { "type": "color_scheme", "id": "color_scheme", "label": "Color scheme", "default": "scheme-1" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Padding" },
    {
